# Copy to server/.env and fill in.
#
# The two connection strings come from Supabase → Project Settings → Database,
# under "Connection string" → "Session pooler". Both carry the database
# password, so treat the file as a credential: it is gitignored, and the value
# should not be pasted through a chat window or a ticket.
#
# Paste each line whole rather than pasting a password into an existing line.
# A selection that runs a few characters too far takes the host with it, and the
# result is a URL that still looks right — `…:<password>.pooler.supabase.com` —
# and fails with an authentication error that sends you looking at the password.
#
# DATABASE_URL is `app_rw`, which row-level security constrains and which the
# server itself runs as.
DATABASE_URL=

# MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL is the owning role (`postgres`), used only by
# scripts/migrate.mjs. It is deliberately a different role from app_rw: the
# server must not be able to drop the policies that contain it.
#
# It does NOT go in this file, and it does not go in .env. Put it in
# server/.env.migrate, which nothing loads automatically — only
# `node scripts/migrate.mjs --production` reads it, and that prints the target
# host and makes you type it back before it connects.
#
# The reason is that every database-backed suite in scripts/ reads
# MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL, and every one of those suites writes. While the
# production value sat in .env, each `npm run check:*` was one forgotten
# override away from the live tenant. For a local run, export it in the shell
# pointing at the local cluster; scripts/local-only.mjs refuses anything else.

# Supabase project details. Tokens are ES256, verified against the published
# public keys — this server holds no signing secret at all, so a leak of
# everything it knows still does not let an attacker mint a token.
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_JWKS_URL=

# NEVER put SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY (sb_secret_...) here or anywhere this app
# reads. It is the service_role equivalent: it bypasses every row-level
# security policy in the schema. Nothing in this codebase wants it.

# The database's CA, so the connection is verified and not merely encrypted.
# Required in production: the server refuses to start without it rather than
# talk to a database whose certificate nobody checked. The file is committed at
# server/certs — it is a public root, not a credential. See certs/README.md.
PGSSLROOTCERT=./certs/supabase-prod-ca-2021.crt

PORT=8080
CORS_ORIGINS=https://harisiva15.github.io
